---
import { site } from '@app/site';
import Prose from '@app/layouts/Prose.astro';
---

<Prose
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What Postal stores, where it stores it, and the few places it connects to."
  updated={site.policyDate}
>
  <p>
    Postal is a mail client that runs on your computer. It has no servers of its own, and its
    developer receives nothing from it: no mail, no addresses, no usage data. This page says what
    Postal stores, where, and what it connects to.
  </p>

  <h2>What Postal stores on your machine</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your mail.</strong> Messages, their bodies and flags are kept in a SQLite Cache under
      <code>~/.cache/postal</code>, so the Message List shows before the server answers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your secrets.</strong> A Google refresh token, or the password of an iCloud or other
      Account, is kept in your operating system's keychain, or in a file in Postal's config
      directory if you choose that in Settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your settings.</strong> <code>~/.config/postal/config.toml</code> holds your Accounts'
      addresses, their servers and your preferences.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unsent mail.</strong> Drafts and Messages waiting in the Outbox are files under
      <code>~/.local/share/postal</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A log.</strong> Errors are written to a file under <code>~/.local/state/postal</code>.
      It never leaves your machine unless you send it to someone.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each directory follows the XDG variable for it when you set one.</p>

  <h2>What Postal connects to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your mail Provider</strong>, over IMAP and SMTP with TLS: to read, send, move and
      delete your mail as you ask.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google's sign-in servers</strong> (<code>accounts.google.com</code>,
      <code>oauth2.googleapis.com</code>), when you sign in to a Google Account and when its access
      token is renewed. You sign in in your own browser; Postal never sees your Google password.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A server lookup</strong>, once, when you sign in to a Provider other than Google or
      iCloud: Postal asks your address's domain, Thunderbird's public server database (
      <code>autoconfig.thunderbird.net</code>) and DNS which IMAP and SMTP servers the domain uses.
      The lookup sends the domain of your address, not the address.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Postal never fetches the images in a Message, so opening mail tells its sender nothing. A link
    opens in your browser only when you choose it.
  </p>

  <h2>Google user data</h2>
  <p>
    To read and send mail in a Google Account, Postal asks for the Gmail scope{' '}
    <code>https://mail.google.com/</code>. It uses that access only to show, search, send and
    organise your mail, on your machine, at your direction.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your Google data is not sent to Postal's developer or to any third party.</li>
    <li>It is not used for advertising, and not used to train AI models.</li>
    <li>No person reads it: it never leaves your machine.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Postal's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other app will adhere
    to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">
      Google API Services User Data Policy
    </a>
    , including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>An agent you run</h2>
  <p>
    The Neovim plugin can open a chat with an AI agent that you install and configure yourself.
    Postal ships no agent and starts none on its own. When you ask your agent about a Message or a
    Draft, its text goes to that agent, and the agent's own privacy terms apply to it. Nothing is
    shared with an agent until you ask.
  </p>

  <h2>No telemetry</h2>
  <p>Postal sends no analytics, no usage statistics and no crash reports.</p>

  <h2>Removing your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Unlink an Account in Settings (<code>,</code>): Postal removes it from the config, deletes its
      secret and its Messages from the Cache.
    </li>
    <li>Delete the directories above to remove everything Postal has stored.</li>
    <li>
      For a Google Account, you can also revoke Postal's access at
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This site is static. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics and loads nothing from third parties;
    its fonts are served from the site itself. It is hosted on GitHub Pages, and GitHub may log the
    IP addresses of visitors, as its own privacy statement describes.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
  <p>
    A change to this policy is published on this page, with its date at the top. Questions go to
    <span class="address">{site.contact}</span>.
  </p>
</Prose>

<style>
  .address {
    font-family: var(--font-code);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    user-select: all;
  }
</style>
